Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:01.670 align:start position:0%
 
we've been talking on today's show about  the road map to easing lockdown  that the prime minister will announce  tomorrow matt hancock said earlier that  it's still being worked on and when i  spoke this week to lord hague the former  foreign secretary and conservative  leader  i asked him what he wants to hear from  boris johnson tomorrow  well i think as with most people i'm  hoping to hear that  before too long the great majority of  restrictions on people can be  lifted and now he's presumably going to  say that that will depend  on the progress that is being made and  that's fair enough  um but i think if we are going to reach  the point  perhaps in april where everybody over  the age of 50  has been has had the opportunity to be  vaccinated  and the number of cases of covid  is down to a very low level the at the  sort of level  we last saw in the middle of the summer  last year if  both of those things have happened by  some time in april  then there wouldn't be much  justification for keeping most of the  restrictions  on people i think with one big caveat  there has to be a deal as it were  between the government and the people of  this country  that we have to do the mass testing when  we reach that point we have to be ready  for really  rapid ruthless local lockdowns  we have to all join making a test and  trace system work which it can do once  the infections are at a lower level  so everybody has to be psychologically  ready for that  and if they are then i think at that  point the government can really release  most of the restrictions and back in  june  you said that lockdown was a disaster  for our society an economic  catastrophe and so destructive that it  can only ever be allowed  once logged as i explained when i wrote  that  article represents the failure of other  policies it's what you do when  every other means of containing a  pandemic has  failed so that means you don't have  effective test and tracing that you  haven't managed to seal the borders that  you haven't managed to  identify through testing the the rise of  the virus  again it's a policy failure really to  have  a lockdown but of course coming in  through 2021 we ought to be in a  position with the development  of mass testing with a test and trace  system that can work at a lower level of  uh  infection and with the huge success of  the vaccination  program great credit to the government  about that  with all of those things well then we do  have the tools now to prevent  future lockdowns i think there are  lessons  now you know and some of them we've just  been talking about  we must have mass testing well that that  couldn't have been developed last summer  um but it can be put all put in place  now  uh the test and trace system has  struggled  but it's partly struggled because  there's still such a high level of  infection to deal with and i think we  have learned that if we  are going to have long terms you have to  do them quickly  the faster you take the action the  better so when it comes  in that this um whatever point it is  later this year that i was talking about  but the government says well you're free  of most restrictions  but we have to be really ruthless and  quick  if the virus rears its head again in a  particular town or city  or locality uh well then they have to be  ready to act  very very fast on those situations or  on the risk of the virus coming back in  again with a mutation from  overseas talking about the way that uh  number 10 has handled  the crisis do you think there's been any  improvement since the changes  of personnel the departure of uh dominic  cummings and lee kane haven't always  been the biggest fans then  i think that has been a big improvement  actually and  i pointed this out recently uh i've not  i don't i don't want to personally  attack the people who left they were  brilliant people but maybe running  government  wasn't their greatest skill compared to  running campaigns  uh well now some people have been  brought in who are very good at running  government you know though the prime  minister has a new chief of staff he's  made other recent appointments  and it's it you can tell over the last  couple of months that  government has worked better there's  been more  under-promising and over-delivering  rather than the other way around there  have been fewer rows  have been fewer destructive leaks and  side briefings to the press you can just  tell you can smell it that  things have improved and the way things  work can come and the public can smell  it  as well one of the big challenges ahead  of course is the budget  what would you like to see in that  budget well the big task for the  chancellor has been  keeping as many people as possible in  work and the economy  ready and intact for the future over the  last year and i think he's done that  very well what is the next task well the  next task is for britain to be at the  forefront  of what i think will be an extraordinary  decade of  innovation coming up uh all over the  world  this is partly a result of the pandemic  partly the result of a technological  race going on between the  the us and china i think a really  important thing to look out for  in the budget is is this country  positioning itself  for that wave of innovation are we going  to have the right  rules and laws and incentives and  cooperation between  government and the private sector and  the right tax  rates to achieve that it's very  important not to disincentivize people  to invest in the businesses of the  future  you know people talk about reforming  capital gains tax well  there may well be some very important  and worthwhile reforms that can be made  of it  but it's really important not to tax  people away  from entrepreneurship from risk-taking  from investing in things that might fail  in the future  as well as all the things that are going  to succeed  so um it's the chancellor's job to work  all that out  um you touched on some of the kind of  global challenges that i'm quite keen to  talk about and  particularly china what kind of  relationship do you think we should be  looking to create  postcoded with china there's obviously  these concerns about human rights abuses  should we be tacking towards the eu  position which is obviously  has china as one of their biggest  trading partners or should we adopting  the american stance more concerned about  china  well if you put it that way i would say  uh more the american  stance but it isn't just a  straightforward a choice  uh as that i think the eu for instance  made a  mistake signing their new investment uh  treaty  with agreement with china just before  the biden administration came in without  consultation with the united states  we actually need western countries to  put their heads together much more on  policy towards china and hopefully  when the prime minister hosts the g7 in  cornwall later this year that sort of  thing  will be on the agenda and can be  achieved but what is the right approach  well i would say it's a hard-headed  approach  it's neither um one of um it's not the  golden age of friendship but it's not  unremitting hostility either it is an  approach where we say well we can't  allow ourselves to be dependent on china  for key technologies in the future that  means we do have to restrict  some of the investments they've tried to  make in this country or the takeover of  british companies  by chinese entities we do have to make  sure for instance all the  the batteries for instance that we need  for electric vehicles in the future  aren't made in china so that we're  dependent on that supply chain  but at the same time we can't solve  global problems without china  we can't stop the next pandemic now we  can't  stop climate change we can't have arms  control  we can't uh deal with a future financial  crisis  without working with china facebook has  blocked  news feeds in australia now this is in  response to this law requiring  tech giants to actually pay news  providers for content  do you think facebook has got this wrong  would you like to see other countries  adopting the australian model of asking  tech  giants to pay news providers for content  social media companies like facebook  have done a lot of good of course in  connecting  people up in in absolutely revolutionary  new ways and the many human benefits of  that  but there's a lot of harm taking place  as well  and that harm includes to the to the  fabric of democracy  uh diminishing for instance uh local  newspapers and traditional news  outlets whose diversity and whose  availability is  is an important part of a free and  democratic society  and particularly feeding polarization  by adjusting news so that it suits the  individual  so that it reinforces the the views of  that individual rather than exposing  them to  wider views so yes i hope other  countries including the uk  will take a similar position we talked a  lot about some of the really big  challenges  um you know facing us at the minute  whether it's china  covid social media companies and  one big thing of course on boris's  johnson's entree is  scotland and the challenge a potential  referendum could cause how concerned are  you  about the prospect of scotland voting  for  independence if there is a referendum  and if there is then boris johnson's not  going to be the person to  lead the campaign for the unionism well  i'm very concerned  about that um i can tell everybody  having been the foreign secretary  of the uk that british standing  in the world the influence of the  british people including the people of  scotland  in the world if we were to break up the  uk  would be very seriously diminished uh  this would be um  a bigger event than than brexit it would  have a bigger impact than anything  whatever your views on on brexit  on the standing of the uk on our ability  to exercise our influence  in the world where we are much more than  the sum of our parts  so i think this would be a catastrophic  development  we got kirstarma on the show at this  weekend as well  um obviously of course there are certain  challenges at the minute for labour  is the government's enjoying a kind of  vaccine bounds if you like  if you've got any advice that you'd give  to a leader of the opposition who is  grappling with a newly elected  government off the back of a landslide  election  it's a very thankless task unless people  think you're about to become  prime minister and people will try and  push you around all the time  uh and and try to we've just seen this  with the speech that kiersta alma gave  the last  few days uh try to over hype what you're  about to say this is a re-launch  you know and uh then you end up taking  part accidentally in a relaunch of  yourself  and then of course everybody's  disappointed and a few weeks later you  have another relaunch  and you end up looking a bit ridiculous  um  so i think sometimes it's better just  keep your head down for a bit  if the government's doing well and let  that wash over you  and still be there uh when the  atmosphere has  changed um thank you so much for being  on the programme mr hager it's  very interesting to talk thank you thank  you very much

